---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const description =
  "Distilled decks on how AMD GPUs actually compute — CDNA / MFMA for data-center silicon, RDNA / WMMA for consumer silicon. Historical hipfire performance snapshots are labeled as such.";
---

<Base title="distil" description={description}>
  <main>
    <section class="hero hero-distil">
      <div class="hero-eyebrow">/learn</div>
      <h1 class="hero-tagline">How AMD GPUs actually compute.</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">
        Short, dense decks on the silicon underneath <code>hipfire</code>. Two
        compute families, two matrix paths, one Rust engine that has to span
        both. The HIP/ROCm side of the conversation that <code>brrr</code>-style
        CUDA explainers don't cover.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-bullets">
        <span>wave32 vs wave64</span>
        <span>WMMA vs MFMA</span>
        <span>GDDR vs HBM</span>
        <span>historical silicon snapshots</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-narrow">
      <div class="distil-deck-list">
        <a class="distil-card" href="/learn/rdna-wmma">
          <div class="distil-card-tag">deck 01 &middot; consumer / pro / APU</div>
          <h2 class="distil-card-title">RDNA &amp; WMMA</h2>
          <p class="distil-card-sub">
            wave32 silicon, GDDR6 / system-RAM, dense 16&times;16&times;16 WMMA on
            both gfx11 and gfx12 (half16 vs half8 operand packing), occupancy
            math, bandwidth roofs, and a dispatch story from RDNA1 (no matrix
            unit) through RDNA4 &mdash; with beta fixture examples across
            gfx1010 &rarr; gfx1201.
          </p>
          <div class="distil-card-meta">
            <span>~11 slides</span>
            <span class="dot">&bull;</span>
            <span>gfx10xx &mdash; gfx12xx</span>
            <span class="dot">&bull;</span>
            <span class="accent">wave32 &middot; occupancy &middot; fixtures</span>
          </div>
        </a>

        <a class="distil-card" href="/learn/cdna-mfma">
          <div class="distil-card-tag">deck 02 &middot; data center</div>
          <h2 class="distil-card-title">CDNA &amp; MFMA</h2>
          <p class="distil-card-sub">
            The Instinct lineage. wave64, HBM3 / HBM3E / HBM4, <code>v_mfma_*</code>
            16&times;16 / 32&times;32 matrix tiles, and a transistor budget that
            throws away the graphics pipeline. From the roofline up through
            CU internals to rocprofv3 metrics.
          </p>
          <div class="distil-card-meta">
            <span>~24 slides</span>
            <span class="dot">&bull;</span>
            <span>gfx908 &mdash; gfx950</span>
            <span class="dot">&bull;</span>
            <span class="accent">MI300X: 304 CUs &middot; 192 GB HBM3</span>
          </div>
        </a>

        <a class="distil-card" href="/learn/why-hipfire">
          <div class="distil-card-tag">deck 03 &middot; the thesis</div>
          <h2 class="distil-card-title">Why hipfire &mdash; RDNA vs NVIDIA</h2>
          <p class="distil-card-sub">
            7900 XTX vs RTX 4090, head-to-head architecture: matrix-tile
            shape, cache topology, register file, and scheduling. A retained
            historical hipfire snapshot reported 69% vs 37% of nominal
            GDDR6X bandwidth; the deck explains the RDNA-native design case.
          </p>
          <div class="distil-card-meta">
            <span>~10 sections</span>
            <span class="dot">&bull;</span>
            <span>gfx1100 vs sm_89</span>
            <span class="dot">&bull;</span>
            <span class="accent">historical: 132 vs 71 tok/s</span>
          </div>
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        Performance figures in these educational decks are historical,
        fixture-bound snapshots, not current baselines or product claims.
        See the <a href="https://github.com/warpfront/hipfire/blob/beta/docs/BENCHMARKS.md">beta benchmark lifecycle and source tables</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-narrow">
      <h2>why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        The CUDA side of the world has a healthy supply of explainers &mdash;
        roofline plots, tensor-core diagrams, NSight walkthroughs. The HIP/ROCm
        side has reference docs but very little of the same kind of
        load-bearing pictorial intuition. Worse, most CUDA explainers conflate
        &ldquo;GPU&rdquo; with &ldquo;NVIDIA datacenter GPU,&rdquo; which silently
        bakes in <strong>wave64, HBM, tensor cores, and CUDA cores</strong> as
        if those were universal. On AMD silicon, two of those four are wrong
        most of the time.
      </p>
      <p>
        These decks are the load-bearing pictures we wanted while writing
        hipfire. Their performance numbers are retained observations from the
        cards used during those campaigns, not current claims, nameplate
        TFLOPs, or vendor marketing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each deck is a self-contained read; they cross-reference each other.
        Start with whichever family you own.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-narrow">
      <h2>the divergence, in one row</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The single most useful framing &mdash; same parent company, same HIP
        source, two fundamentally different chips.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th></th>
              <th>CDNA &mdash; Instinct</th>
              <th>RDNA &mdash; Radeon / Ryzen AI</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>wavefront</strong></td>
              <td>64 threads</td>
              <td>32 threads</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>matrix unit</strong></td>
              <td><code>v_mfma_*</code></td>
              <td><code>v_wmma_*</code> (RDNA3+)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>memory</strong></td>
              <td>HBM3 / HBM3E / HBM4</td>
              <td>GDDR6 / GDDR6X / system RAM</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>graphics pipeline</strong></td>
              <td class="warn">none</td>
              <td class="ok">full</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>LLVM target</strong></td>
              <td><code>gfx908 &rarr; gfx950</code></td>
              <td><code>gfx1010 &rarr; gfx1201</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>typical user</strong></td>
              <td>cloud / HPC / lab</td>
              <td>desktop / workstation / iGPU</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="footnote">
        The same <code>.hip</code> source compiles for either &mdash; but ported
        kernels hit three landmines: wavefront width, matrix intrinsic, and
        memory tier sizing. The decks walk each one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section section-narrow">
      <h2>related reading</h2>
      <ul class="doclinks">
        <li><a href="/docs/architecture">/docs/architecture</a> &mdash; the engine layout these decks describe</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/benchmarks">/docs/benchmarks</a> &mdash; live community rows and fixture-scoped beta snapshots</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/quantization">/docs/quantization</a> &mdash; what the WMMA &amp; MFMA kernels actually consume</li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/warpfront/hipfire/tree/beta">github.com/warpfront/hipfire</a> &mdash; engine source</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
